>> Memory in JavaScript

JavaScript uses memory to store variables, objects, functions, and data while your program runs. The JavaScript engine manages memory automatically through allocation, usage, and garbage collection.

1. Memory Allocation
When you create variables, JavaScript allocates memory for them.

let name = "Rahul";
let age = 25;
let user = {
  city: "Bangalore"
};
"Rahul" and 25 are primitive values.
{ city: "Bangalore" } is an object.

2. Stack Memory
The stack is used for:
-> Primitive values
-> Function calls (execution contexts)
-> References to objects

Example:
let a = 10;
let b = a;
Stack
-----
a = 10
b = 10

When b changes, a is unaffected because primitives are copied by value.

3. Heap Memory
The heap stores:

Objects
Arrays
Functions

Example:
let user1 = {
  name: "Rahul"
};

let user2 = user1;

Stack
-----
user1 ---> Address A
user2 ---> Address A

Heap
----
Address A:
{ name: "Rahul" }

Both variables point to the same object.

4. Function Memory (Call Stack)
Every function call gets its own stack frame.

function greet() {
  let message = "Hello";
  console.log(message);
}

greet();

Execution:

Call Stack
----------
greet()
Global

After greet() finishes, its memory is removed from the stack.

5. Garbage Collection
JavaScript automatically removes unused memory.

let person = {
  name: "Rahul"
};

person = null;

The object is no longer reachable, so it becomes eligible for garbage collection.

>> Memory Lifecycle
1. Allocate Memory
       ↓
2. Use Memory
       ↓
3. Release Memory (Garbage Collection)

Example:
let arr = [1, 2, 3]; // allocate

console.log(arr[0]); // use

arr = null;          // eligible for cleanup

>> Quick Interview Answer

>> How memory works in JavaScript?

JavaScript stores primitive values in stack memory.
Objects, arrays, and functions are stored in heap memory.
Variables that reference objects store the object's memory address.
Function calls are managed using the call stack.
Unused memory is automatically cleaned by the garbage collector.

Example
let x = 10;               // Stack

let obj = { value: 10 };  // Reference in Stack, Object in Heap
Stack                    Heap
-----                    ----
x = 10

obj -----> Address1 ---> { value: 10 }

This stack-vs-heap model is the most commonly expected explanation in JavaScript interviews.
